WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:04.830
While plenty of films have taken a stab at
putting ripped-from-the-headlines horror on

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:08.660
the big screen, some are better than others
at capturing the look of real-life serial

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:09.200
killers.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:11.179
Jeffrey Dahmer

00:00:11.179 --> 00:00:17.960
Between 1987 and 1991, Jeffrey Dahmer lured
16 men to his apartment, fed them sleeping

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:21.140
pills, and disposed of them in all kinds of
horrific ways.

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:26.870
When Dahmer was arrested on June 22, 1991,
the police found body parts in his refrigerator

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:30.450
and a half-finished shrine made from skeletal
remains.

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:35.270
Jeremy Renner, who portrayed the Milwaukee
Cannibal in the 2002 biopic Dahmer, reportedly

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:37.590
didn't have a lot of time to prepare for his
role.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:38.970
He told Interview Magazine,

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:42.840
"I didn't even know who Jeffrey Dahmer was,
and then four days later after reading the

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:46.810
script we're going into principal photography,
and two weeks later we're done."

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:50.440
Nevertheless, Renner's performance and appearance
were both eerily convincing.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:52.460
Aileen Wuornos

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:58.380
Charlize Theron's Oscar-winning take on highway
killer Aileen Wuornos in 2003's Monster is

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:01.989
as bleak as it gets, but the real story is
even bleaker.

00:01:01.989 --> 00:01:07.740
In 1989 and 1990, Wuornos lured men into the
woods in central Florida and shot them point-blank

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:08.740
with a pistol.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:13.220
In less than 12 months, she shot seven men
and pawned their belongings in nearby cities,

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:14.810
leaving their bodies in the woods.

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:19.810
Theron gained 30 pounds for the role and endured
painstaking sessions in the makeup chair to

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:22.990
have latex and dental prosthetics applied
before filming.

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:27.510
To match Wuornos' mannerisms, she watched
documentaries about the killer and read through

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:31.220
12 years' worth of personal correspondence
that Wuornos had sent from jail.

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:33.820
Ed Gein

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:37.920
Despite being the basis for plenty of film
killers, such as Buffalo Bill in The Silence

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:42.570
of the Lambs and Leatherface in The Texas
Chainsaw Massacre, the real-life Ed Gein only

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:43.850
killed two people.

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:48.320
What he did a little more often than killing
was dig up corpses and make clothes and lampshades

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:51.369
out of their remains, all because he wanted
to look like his mother.

00:01:51.369 --> 00:01:55.890
With a story like that, it's no wonder Gein
inspired so many screen killers, although

00:01:55.890 --> 00:02:00.780
the 2000 biopic Ed Gein is one of the few
films that focuses on the actual man behind

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:02.200
the macabre crimes.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:08.060
Gein is played by Steve Railsback, who also
portrayed Charles Manson in 1976's Helter

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:09.060
Skelter.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:12.410
This isn't a typically sensational story about
trying to stop the killer before he kills

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:15.500
again, but it definitely dives into disturbing
territory.

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:17.540
John Wayne Gacy

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:21.810
Dubbed the "The Killer Clown," John Wayne
Gacy was convicted of ending the lives of

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:27.489
33 teenage boys and young men between 1972
and 1978 in Cook County, Illinois.

00:02:27.489 --> 00:02:31.319
He was known for burying his victims in the
crawl space under his house, and his nickname

00:02:31.319 --> 00:02:36.200
came from his appearances as "Pogo the Clown"
at fundraising events, parades, and children's

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:37.220
parties.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:41.360
Plenty of fictional characters were inspired
by Gacy's clown act, but surprisingly few

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:43.580
films have been made about the man himself.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:48.800
The 2003 biopic Gacy, also known as The Crawl
Space, is one exception that made an effort

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.319
to depict the killer's life in a realistic
way.

00:02:51.319 --> 00:02:55.670
Gacy is played by Mark Holton, who matches
the killer most closely in terms of body type

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:56.480
and makeup.

00:02:57.220 --> 00:02:59.200
Jack Kevorkian

00:02:59.209 --> 00:03:02.670
Was Jack Kevorkian a serial killer or an agent
of mercy?

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:06.970
Even today, the debate rages on about the
physician the media dubbed "Dr. Death" for

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:08.620
championing euthanasia.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:12.849
In You Don't Know Jack, Al Pacino plays a
man who fights the social norm to give people

00:03:12.849 --> 00:03:14.769
the option to choose their own deaths.

00:03:14.769 --> 00:03:19.750
The HBO film won the actor an Emmy, a Golden
Globe, and a Screen Actors Guild Award, and

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:21.170
it's easy to see why.

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:24.860
Although Pacino admitted that he never met
Kevorkian, who was still alive at the time

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:29.409
of the film's 2010 release, he had plenty
of televised footage to base his performance

00:03:29.409 --> 00:03:30.409
on.

00:03:30.409 --> 00:03:34.340
Everything from his glasses to his mannerisms
to his courtroom rants matched up closely

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:35.640
with the real Kevorkian.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.080
Karla Homolka

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:43.040
The 2006 crime thriller Karla is a brutal
film told from the point of view of Karla

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:47.670
Homolka, a Canadian woman who killed three
women with her husband Paul Bernardo in the

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:48.680
early '90s.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:50.710
The two were arrested in 1993.

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:54.849
Bernardo was sentenced to life in prison,
while Karla spent 12 years in custody and

00:03:54.849 --> 00:03:56.500
was released in 2005.

00:03:56.500 --> 00:04:01.019
In the film, the blonde-haired Karla is played
by Laura Prepon, who is probably best known

00:04:01.019 --> 00:04:02.570
as Donna from That '70s Show.

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:07.849
Prepon may be a natural redhead, but her performance
as Homolka is nonetheless fairly convincing.

